{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794755078,"nonce":"AaKbWS0phXdjo9oJKaP3Wokf5Zk=","title":"hxcpp on wasm: what a binding adds to the module, and the spill-pointers pass","message":"Measured on 2026-11-15 on hxn-gpu-webgpu's check (68 native calls), Rust release, hxcpp from git (615adc73) at its default -O2 link, emsdk 5.0.7, linked with --profiling-funcs and -D nostrip to keep names; code-section bytes by demangled namespace.\n\n| Part | With spill-pointers | Without (HXCPP_NO_GC_LINK) |\n|---|---|---|\n| hxcpp runtime, Haxe std, libc, Emscripten runtime | 736 KB | 422 KB |\n| generated native call wrappers (HxnGpuWebgpuNative) | 173 KB | 95 KB |\n| generated Haxe classes and data | 103 KB | 63 KB |\n| generated C++ glue | 76 KB | 46 KB |\n| Rust std, core, alloc | 65 KB | 47 KB |\n| hxn, hxn-future, krio | 48 KB | 28 KB |\n| the driver and its adapter | 27 KB | 15 KB |\n| emdawnwebgpu's webgpu.cpp | 16 KB | 11 KB |\n| the check program | 156 KB | 62 KB |\n| total | 1411 KB | 797 KB |\n\nA bare hxcpp program (Sys.println) is 596 KB of wasm and 22 KB of JS; the check is 1.54 MB and 118 KB (the port's library_webgpu.js is most of the difference in JS).\n\n- The spill-pointers pass, which hxcpp's collector needs on wasm, instruments every function in the module, Rust and webgpu.cpp included: it accounts for 614 KB of the 1411.\n- hxn's generated code is about 350 KB with it, 204 KB without: per native call about 2.5 KB of wrapper, 1.1 KB of glue and 1.5 KB of class code.\n- @:unreflective on the native class removes the STATIC_HX_DEFINE_DYNAMIC_FUNC thunks and saves 19 KB of 1411; most of the wrapper's size is what is inlined into it (the error-buffer check that throws, and hxnOwn), not reflection.\n\nTo try: @:unreflective on the generated native class (it is never called dynamically); the error path of hxn::check out of line so each wrapper keeps only the status test; limiting the spill pass to hxcpp's own objects, if Binaryen allows it per function, since Rust code holds no GC pointers.","files":null}]}